  "author": "",
  "attributionNote": "Belonging to the Taittirīya school of the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda and transmitted without an author's name: the śikṣā, brahmānanda and bhṛgu vallīs of the school's āraṇyaka. GRETIL's e-text was input by Ivan Andrijanić.",
  "description": "The Taittirīya Upaniṣad opens with the rules of the teacher and the pupil — truth, austerity, self-control, the sacrifice, hospitality, and the call to keep the line of the Vedas unbroken — and moves through the five sheaths of the self to the teaching given to Bhrigu: the knower of Brahman attains the highest, and Brahman is that from which beings are born, by which they live and into which they return. It is the Upanishad of the pupil's obligations and of the joy of the one who has understood.",
  "historicalContext": "The Upanishad of the Yajurveda's Taittirīya school, read with the school's āraṇyaka and given its place in the Advaita curriculum by the commentary attributed to Śaṅkara; its chapters on conduct make it the Upanishad most often read at the beginning of a course of study.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete as GRETIL publishes it: the whole Upaniṣad in three vallīs, 51 passages, streamed from GRETIL at read time. The file also carries Śaṅkara's commentary on part of the text — the fourth anuvākas of the third vallī have no commentary in this transcription — so the commentary is not shown as a second pane and the reader's notice names the edition being read.",
